   "subject": "Re: [r-inla] How can I obtain the over-dipersion parameter in r-inla?",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Hi,<div><br></div><div>Maybe this article can be interesting for you:</div><div><br></div><div><a href=\"https://www.raco.cat/index.php/SORT/article/view/302264\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://www.raco.cat/index.php/SORT/article/view/302264&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792442352424000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1bTYzBvtjjvRs3_Jl9y2Ox\">https://www.raco.cat/index.php/SORT/article/view/302264</a><br></div><div><br></div><div>Regards,</div><div><br></div><div>Martí</div></div></div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_attr\">Missatge de l&#39;adreça &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">attn...@gmail.com</a>&gt; del dia dl., 18 de març 2019 a les 5:05:<br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\">Thanks for the suggestion to use family = &quot;nbinomial&quot; to obtain the overdispersion parameter. Could you suggest a threshold for the parameter as to what would be considered too &quot;overdispersed&quot; to apply a poisson model (or point me in the direction of a paper that could)?</blockquote><div><br></div><div> </div><br>On Saturday, 3 March 2012 02:51:30 UTC-7, INLA help  wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\">On Fri, 2012-03-02 at 11:47 -0800, Martí Casals wrote:<p>&gt; I fitted a Poisson mixed model with “Nwrestler” as random effect. The<br>&gt; model is overdispersed. For this reason, I obtained the Pearson<br>&gt; dispersion parameter with the lme4 package (Phi=1.56). </p><p><br>&gt; When I’ve used the inla package, I&#39;ve obtained the variance of random<br>&gt; effect but I don&#39;t know how can obtain the dispersion parameter. </p><p>You don&#39;t. Spesify instead a likelihood which allow for overdispersion,<br>f.ex the negative binomial in your case (family = &quot;nbinomial&quot;). Then you<br>get an estimate of the overdispersion parameter in that model; see<br>inla.doc(&quot;nbinomial&quot;)</p><p>Another alternative, is to add a iid term to each linear predictor,<br>which will increase the variance. But it can be difficult to relate the<br>variance/precision of this term, directly to the &#39;dispersion parameter&#39;<br>you get out from lme4. </p><p><br>-- <br>INLA help &lt;<a rel=\"nofollow\">he...@r-inla.org</a>&gt;<br>R-INLA</p><p></p><p></p><p></p><p></p><p></p><p></p></blockquote></div>\r\n\r\n<p></p></blockquote></div>"
    ],
